---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteFoot from '../components/SiteFoot.astro';
---

<Base
    title="Projects — Samuel Woo"
    description="Projects by Samuel Woo — things I'm building, with a few write-ups on how they came together."
    page="projects"
    skipTarget="projects"
>
    <main class="content">

        {/* PROJECTS HERO */}
        <section id="projects" class="band band-projects-hero" aria-labelledby="projects-title">
            <div class="band-inner">
                {/* Line-draw motif echoing the home hero's sage stroke register
                     — shapes the right-side space. (The home hero itself is a
                     canvas now; this is the last hand-drawn one.) */}
                <svg class="projects-motif" viewBox="0 0 400 300" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                    {/* Inner <g> is the GSAP parallax handle; anime.js still draws
                         the .motif-draw strokes. Two libraries, two properties. */}
                    <g class="motif-parallax">
                        <path class="motif-draw" d="M60 150 C 120 60, 200 60, 220 150 C 240 240, 320 240, 340 150" />
                        <path class="motif-draw" d="M40 210 L 360 210" />
                        <circle class="motif-draw" cx="220" cy="90" r="8" />
                        <path class="motif-draw" d="M300 70 L 330 100 M330 70 L 300 100" />
                    </g>
                </svg>
                <p class="kicker reveal reveal-clip">Projects</p>
                <h1 id="projects-title" class="section-title reveal reveal-clip">Things I'm building</h1>
                <p class="section-intro reveal">A running list of what I'm building, mostly outside of work — some shipped, some barely past a napkin sketch. A couple have write-ups on how they came together; the rest are still cooking.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        {/* CASE STUDY 01 — Finance dashboard */}
        <section class="band case-study" aria-labelledby="cs-finance-title">
            <div class="band-inner">
                {/* Marginalia read-progress rail. Purely decorative (aria-hidden),
                     lives in the band's own left padding at wide widths and is
                     display:none below 1280px, so it can never cause overflow.
                     `position: sticky` in styles.css is what holds it beside the
                     prose; gsap-motion.js only scrubs the fill (its §5c explains
                     why ScrollTrigger's pin:true was rejected — a pin-spacer in
                     the flow is a layout-shift risk this page does not need).
                     Its CSS resting state is a fully-drawn rail, so no-JS /
                     reduced-motion reads as finished artwork rather than an
                     empty track. */}
                <aside class="case-rail" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span class="case-rail-track"><span class="case-rail-fill"></span><span class="case-rail-head"></span></span>
                </aside>
                <div class="case-head reveal">
                    <span class="case-index" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
                    <div class="case-head-main">
                        <h2 id="cs-finance-title" class="case-title">Ka-ching — Personal Finance Dashboard</h2>
                        <p class="case-tagline">A local Mint/Monarch alternative that turns a pile of bank statements into one honest picture of where the money went.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <ul class="case-meta reveal">
                    <li><span class="case-meta-k">Role</span><span class="case-meta-v">Solo — design &amp; build</span></li>
                    <li><span class="case-meta-k">Stack</span><span class="case-meta-v">Python · Vanilla JS · HTML/CSS</span></li>
                    <li><span class="case-meta-k">Status</span><span class="case-meta-v">Working &amp; in daily use</span></li>
                </ul>

                <div class="case-body">
                    <div class="case-block reveal gsap-reveal">
                        <h3 class="case-sub">Why I built it</h3>
                        <p>Mint shut down, the alternatives all want a subscription and a linked login to every account I own, and none of them ever categorized my spending the way I actually think about it. I wanted the full picture — cash flow, trends, trips, a budget that talks to my auto-invest plan — without handing my credentials to anyone. So I built it to run entirely on my own machine.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="case-block reveal gsap-reveal">
                        <h3 class="case-sub">How it works</h3>
                        <p>You drop statements into a folder and it figures out the rest. Content-detected parsers read Chase checking, Chase Sapphire Preferred, Capital One Venture X, and Venmo, and normalize all of them into one ledger — so a coffee is a coffee whether it came from a PDF, a CSV, or an OFX export. Every transaction gets categorized by a rules engine I can teach, and the dashboard renders spending, a cash-flow Sankey, trends, per-trip breakdowns, and a coupled budget + auto-invest planner.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="case-block reveal gsap-reveal">
                        <h3 class="case-sub">The part I'm proud of</h3>
                        <p>The parsing. Bank statements are gloriously inconsistent, and getting a PDF, a CSV, and an OFX file to agree on what a transaction <em>is</em> took more care than the whole front end. It content-detects the source instead of trusting file names, falls back through a few PDF extractors when the nice one isn't installed, and has a reconcile step that catches when an import double-counts. It's the least glamorous part and the reason I actually trust the numbers.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="case-block reveal gsap-reveal">
                        <h3 class="case-sub">What I'd do next</h3>
                        <p>More parsers, mostly — every new account is a new statement format to teach it. And I keep eyeing a proper import-review UI so adding a new bank doesn't mean reading my own code six months later.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <ul class="bubble-list case-tags reveal" aria-label="Highlights">
                    <li class="bubble">Runs 100% locally</li>
                    <li class="bubble">Multi-format parsing</li>
                    <li class="bubble">Cash-flow Sankey</li>
                    <li class="bubble">Budget + auto-invest</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        {/* CASE STUDY 02 — Portfolio site */}
        {/* Inverted. Two consecutive case studies in the same register read
             as one long slab; alternating the plate turns them into two
             distinct spreads and gives this page the same
             light / dark / inset rhythm as the home page. `.band--dark`
             redefines the design tokens for its own subtree, so the
             case-rail, the meta strip and the prose all follow with no
             markup change. */}
        <section class="band case-study band--dark" aria-labelledby="cs-site-title">
            <div class="band-inner">
                <aside class="case-rail" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span class="case-rail-track"><span class="case-rail-fill"></span><span class="case-rail-head"></span></span>
                </aside>
                <div class="case-head reveal">
                    <span class="case-index" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
                    <div class="case-head-main">
                        <h2 id="cs-site-title" class="case-title">This Website</h2>
                        <p class="case-tagline">A personal site for the stuff that doesn't fit on a résumé — and an excuse to actually enjoy front-end for once.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <ul class="case-meta reveal">
                    <li><span class="case-meta-k">Role</span><span class="case-meta-v">Solo — design &amp; build</span></li>
                    <li><span class="case-meta-k">Stack</span><span class="case-meta-v">Astro · One React island · Hand-written CSS</span></li>
                    <li><span class="case-meta-k">Status</span><span class="case-meta-v">Live — you're on it</span></li>
                </ul>

                <div class="case-body">
                    <div class="case-block reveal gsap-reveal">
                        <h3 class="case-sub">Why I built it</h3>
                        <p>Most of my day is spent deep in robotics middleware where nobody sees the interface. A UI I built for visualizing robot navigation reminded me how much I like the front-end side of things, so I wanted a small space that was entirely mine to design — no spec, no product manager, no ticket.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="case-block reveal gsap-reveal">
                        <h3 class="case-sub">How it's built</h3>
                        <p>It started as three hand-written HTML files and outgrew them — the head, the nav and the atmosphere layer were triplicated verbatim across all three, until the copy-paste was the bug rather than the point. So there's a build step now: Astro, static output, one layout, components as .astro files, and plain prerendered HTML by the time it reaches you. The CSS never moved — still hand-written, still served as-is, split across five sheets by domain (layout, nav, scroll, motion, and the original one the rest grew out of) so each has a single owner, with no scoping and no class hashing. There's exactly one React island, for the one thing here that genuinely needs state: the contour drawing behind my name on the home page is a putting green, and you can putt on it. Animation is GSAP and anime.js under four JS files of my own — one of those replaces Motion with a reimplementation of the four APIs the site actually used, which took a 136KB vendor bundle off the page.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="case-block reveal gsap-reveal">
                        <h3 class="case-sub">The details I cared about</h3>
                        <p>The motion is all there to serve the content, not show off — scroll reveals, a hero that pins and parallaxes as you scroll past it, a soft spotlight that tracks your cursor — and every bit of it is switched off cleanly when a visitor prefers reduced motion. The green isn't decoration pretending to be a game, either: the ball reads the gradient of the same height field the contour lines are drawn from, so reading the break genuinely works, and an offline sweep of every aim and power is what tells me the holes it generates are sinkable in one putt. I held it to real accessibility standards: keyboard-navigable, AA color contrast measured off the rendered pixels rather than the DOM, focus states you can actually see. The look leans on a calm sage palette because I wanted it to feel like a gallery, not a landing page.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="case-block reveal gsap-reveal">
                        <h3 class="case-sub">What I'd do next</h3>
                        <p>Keep it alive. It's the kind of project that's never really finished — a new section here, a nicer transition there, whenever I've got a free evening and an idea.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <ul class="bubble-list case-tags reveal" aria-label="Highlights">
                    <li class="bubble">Playable hero green</li>
                    <li class="bubble">One React island</li>
                    <li class="bubble">Reduced-motion aware</li>
                    <li class="bubble">WCAG AA contrast</li>
                    <li class="bubble">Hand-written CSS</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        {/* STILL COOKING (teasers) */}
        {/* Inset plate, closing the page the way the home page's projects
             band does — the teasers sit on their own sheet rather than
             bleeding to the viewport edge. */}
        <section class="band band--shapes band--plate" aria-labelledby="cooking-title">
            {/* Line-art "simmering pot" abstraction for the still-cooking band */}
            <svg class="shape-field shape-field--cooking" viewBox="0 0 240 240" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                <g class="shape-drift">
                    <path class="shape-line" d="M56 128 L 184 128 L 172 208 L 68 208 Z" />
                    <path class="shape-line" d="M40 128 L 200 128" />
                    <path class="shape-line" d="M96 106 C 96 84, 78 80, 88 58 C 96 42, 84 34, 84 24" />
                    <path class="shape-line" d="M144 106 C 144 82, 162 78, 152 56 C 144 40, 156 32, 156 22" />
                    <circle class="shape-dot" cx="84" cy="24" r="4" />
                    <circle class="shape-dot shape-dot--soft" cx="156" cy="22" r="4.5" />
                </g>
            </svg>
            <div class="band-inner" data-rail="03 / On the bench">
                <div class="section-head">
                    <p class="kicker reveal reveal-clip">Also on the bench</p>
                    <h2 id="cooking-title" class="section-title reveal reveal-clip">Still cooking</h2>
                    <p class="section-intro reveal">Ideas I'm poking at, and a couple of older things I never wrote up. Check back.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="section-body">
                    <div class="project-grid project-grid--even">
                        <article class="card project-card reveal spotlight" aria-labelledby="teaser-dating-title">
                            <span class="project-index" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
                            <h3 id="teaser-dating-title" class="card-title">Restaurant Dating App</h3>
                            <p class="project-tech">Concept · Mobile App</p>
                            <p>Matching people on their taste in restaurants instead of the usual profile — think Hinge meets Beli. The best first dates happen over good food anyway.</p>
                        </article>

                        <article class="card project-card reveal spotlight" aria-labelledby="teaser-navui-title">
                            <span class="project-index" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
                            <h3 id="teaser-navui-title" class="card-title">Navigation Orchestrator UI</h3>
                            <p class="project-tech">Frontend · Work Project</p>
                            <p>A UI for controlling and visualizing robot navigation in simulation. The project that reminded me I actually like front-end — and the reason this site exists.</p>
                        </article>

                        <article class="card project-card reveal spotlight" aria-labelledby="teaser-imu-title">
                            <span class="project-index" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
                            <h3 id="teaser-imu-title" class="card-title">IMU Activity Classification</h3>
                            <p class="project-tech">Python · Keras · ROS · C++</p>
                            <p>Trained 10+ CNNs on IMU sensor data to 90% accuracy, then ran real-time classification on-device in about a second and a half. My first taste of ML on real hardware.</p>
                        </article>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <SiteFoot right="back" />
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
</Base>
